\chapter{Cartesian Kerr--Schild Coordinates and Event Horizon Regularity}
\label{ch:kerr_schild}

\section{Singularity Inversion in Boyer--Lindquist Spacetime}
In standard relativistic computations, the spinning Kerr black hole is frequently cast in Boyer--Lindquist coordinates $(t, r, \theta, \phi)$. While geometrically intuitive at asymptotic infinity, the metric tensor exhibits a coordinate breakdown across the outer event horizon:
\begin{equation}
    r_+ = M + \sqrt{M^2 - a^2}
\end{equation}
where the radial lapse function $\Delta(r) = r^2 - 2Mr + a^2$ vanishes, inducing singular coordinate velocities $dr/dt \to 0$ and producing catastrophic underflow in numerical ODE solvers.

\section{The Cartesian Kerr--Schild Null Congruence}
To circumvent coordinate singularities and guarantee smooth numerical traversal of null geodesics across the horizon, we formulate the entire computational manifold within Cartesian Kerr--Schild coordinates $(t, x, y, z)$. In this formulation, the metric is decomposed as:
\begin{equation}
    g_{\mu\nu} = \eta_{\mu\nu} + 2 H k_\mu k_\nu, \qquad g^{\mu\nu} = \eta^{\mu\nu} - 2 H k^\mu k^\nu
\end{equation}
where $\eta_{\mu\nu} = \operatorname{diag}(-1, 1, 1, 1)$, and $k_\mu$ is a null vector satisfying:
\begin{equation}
    k^\mu k_\mu = g^{\mu\nu} k_\mu k_\nu = \eta^{\mu\nu} k_\mu k_\nu = 0
\end{equation}

The scalar potential $H(x,y,z)$ and the covariant null congruence $k_\mu$ are expressed explicitly in terms of the auxiliary ellipsoidal radius $r$:
\begin{align}
    H(x, y, z) &= \frac{M r^3}{r^4 + a^2 z^2} \\
    k_\mu &= \left( 1, \frac{r x + a y}{r^2 + a^2}, \frac{r y - a x}{r^2 + a^2}, \frac{z}{r} \right)
\end{align}

The auxiliary radius $r$ is extracted unambiguously via the root of the quadric:
\begin{equation}
    r^2 = \frac{1}{2} \left[ (R^2 - a^2) + \sqrt{(R^2 - a^2)^2 + 4 a^2 z^2} \right], \quad R^2 = x^2 + y^2 + z^2
\end{equation}
Because $H$ remains finite everywhere outside the physical ring singularity ($r=0, z=0$), the inverse metric components $g^{\mu\nu}$ remain differentiable $C^\infty$ across $r = r_+$.

